1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,480
This is ECE 320, video 22a: multicore, part 1. It covers pages 1 to 55 of unit 12, shared memory multiprocessors.

2
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:20,845
After this video you can do four things. One: explain threads, hardware multithreading and the shared memory model.

3
00:00:21,295 --> 00:00:26,265
Two: trace two threads that share memory, and find the final value in memory.

4
00:00:26,715 --> 00:00:33,094
Three: find the order of instructions that breaks a lock, and fix the lock with an atomic swap.

5
00:00:33,544 --> 00:00:39,944
Four: show why private caches can return an old value. This is the cache coherence problem.

6
00:00:40,394 --> 00:00:50,805
Unit 12 has five topics: thread-level parallelism, the shared memory model, synchronization, cache coherence, and memory consistency models.

7
00:00:51,255 --> 00:01:02,476
This video covers the first three, and the start of cache coherence: why private caches cause a problem. Video 22b covers the coherence protocols.

8
00:01:02,926 --> 00:01:08,238
Video 22c covers the last topic, memory consistency models.

9
00:01:08,688 --> 00:01:20,229
There are five reasons to build multiprocessors. Reason 1 is performance. Instruction-level parallelism is limited, so the performance of one core is limited.

10
00:01:20,679 --> 00:01:29,405
Reason 2 is power efficiency. More cores at a lower clock frequency give more throughput, but maybe a longer latency.

11
00:01:29,855 --> 00:01:38,303
Reason 3 is cost. Big systems are built from ordinary cores, and a chip has enough transistors for many cores.

12
00:01:38,753 --> 00:01:47,009
Reasons 4 and 5: an upgrade can add cores, and if one processor fails, the other N minus 1 still work.

13
00:01:47,459 --> 00:01:55,245
On one processor, concurrency comes from software threads. A thread runs a stream of instructions of a program.

14
00:01:55,695 --> 00:02:03,994
Each thread has private state: its context, which is the PC and the registers, and its own stack for local variables.

15
00:02:04,444 --> 00:02:12,956
Shared state, like global variables and the heap, is the same for all threads. The threads share one memory address space.

16
00:02:13,406 --> 00:02:18,249
A software process is like a thread, but it has its own memory space.

17
00:02:18,699 --> 00:02:27,744
The operating system manages the threads. In a single-core system, all threads share one processor. Thread 0 runs first.

18
00:02:28,194 --> 00:02:37,154
A hardware timer interrupt triggers the OS. The OS saves thread 0 and loads thread 1. This is a context switch.

19
00:02:37,604 --> 00:02:49,593
The OS keeps swapping. Quick swaps give the illusion of concurrent execution. Rule: threads share memory, and each thread has its own PC, registers and stack.

20
00:02:50,043 --> 00:03:00,113
Now run two threads on hardware. The first way is the multiplexed uniprocessor: one core, and the OS or runtime swaps the threads.

21
00:03:00,563 --> 00:03:06,685
The threads interleave in time, but they never run at the same time. There is no parallelism.

22
00:03:07,135 --> 00:03:18,975
The second way is multiprocessing. The simplest multiprocessor replicates the entire processor pipeline: two PCs, two register files, two ALUs.

23
00:03:19,425 --> 00:03:30,113
The exception: here both pipelines share the instruction cache and the data cache. The shared cache becomes a bottleneck, and the end of this video comes back to it.

24
00:03:30,563 --> 00:03:37,347
Now each thread has its own pipeline, so both threads run at the same time. This is parallelism.

25
00:03:37,797 --> 00:03:47,333
Both pipelines load and store through the shared data cache to one memory. Their memory accesses interleave. This is the shared memory model.

26
00:03:47,783 --> 00:03:54,119
The third way is hardware multithreading. Several threads share one pipeline, with its caches.

27
00:03:54,569 --> 00:04:05,151
The hardware replicates only the thread context: one PC and one register file per thread. The select signal, T H R, picks the thread.

28
00:04:05,601 --> 00:04:19,787
The reason: one program often leaves the pipeline idle. In this example, thread 0 issues in cycles 1 to 3, then waits for a cache miss. It issues in the same cycles in both rows.

29
00:04:20,237 --> 00:04:31,651
Thread 0 issues again in cycles 10 and 11. It uses 5 of 12 slots. This is under 50 percent, as the slides say for single programs.

30
00:04:32,101 --> 00:04:37,199
With multithreading, thread 1 issues in the slots that thread 0 leaves empty.

31
00:04:37,649 --> 00:04:43,452
Now all 12 slots are used. So multithreading improves utilization and throughput.

32
00:04:43,902 --> 00:04:52,947
Thread 0 still finishes in cycle 11, so it is not faster. A thread can even run slower, because it shares the pipeline.

33
00:04:53,397 --> 00:05:02,592
Rule: multithreading improves utilization and throughput, but not single-thread performance. It has the same shared memory model.

34
00:05:03,042 --> 00:05:13,581
Processors can communicate in two ways. With message passing, a processor sends explicit messages, for example between the computers of a cluster.

35
00:05:14,031 --> 00:05:22,841
With shared memory, processors communicate through one shared address space, with loads and stores. The course uses this model.

36
00:05:23,291 --> 00:05:34,193
Shared memory has three related issues. Issue 1 is synchronization: how to regulate access to shared data with critical sections. This video covers it.

37
00:05:34,643 --> 00:05:46,184
Issue 2 is cache coherence: how a write to a location shows up to the other processors. This video shows the problem, and video 22b the protocols.

38
00:05:46,634 --> 00:05:58,858
Issue 3 is the memory consistency model: how to keep shared memory simple while the hardware optimizes, for example with store buffers. Video 22c covers it.

39
00:05:59,308 --> 00:06:07,905
The example is a bank. A loop goes over all transactions. Each transaction has an account id and an amount to debit.

40
00:06:08,355 --> 00:06:13,390
A transaction debits the account only if the balance is at least the amount.

41
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:24,315
Different transactions can run in parallel. This is thread-level parallelism: a collection of asynchronous tasks that are not started and stopped together.

42
00:06:24,765 --> 00:06:37,522
One transaction compiles to five RISC-V instructions. x 1 holds the id, and x 2 the amount. Instruction 0 puts the address of the balance into x 3.

43
00:06:37,972 --> 00:06:47,039
The array accts is shared, so the compiler cannot keep a balance in a register. Instruction 1 loads the balance into x 4.

44
00:06:47,489 --> 00:06:55,958
Instruction 2 skips the debit if the balance is less than the amount. Instruction 3 subtracts the amount in x 4.

45
00:06:56,408 --> 00:06:59,928
Instruction 4 stores the new balance back to memory.

46
00:07:00,378 --> 00:07:09,253
Rule: a thread reads shared data with a load and writes it with a store. Between them, the value is a private copy in a register.

47
00:07:09,703 --> 00:07:22,823
Two people withdraw 100 dollars from account 241 at two ATMs. Each transaction runs as a thread on its own processor. The balance starts at 500.

48
00:07:23,273 --> 00:07:32,169
Thread 0 starts. Instruction 0 only computes the address of the balance into x 3, so memory does not change.

49
00:07:32,619 --> 00:07:41,749
Instruction 1 loads the balance. The value 500 travels from memory into x 4 of thread 0: a private copy.

50
00:07:42,199 --> 00:07:50,327
Instruction 2 compares: 500 is not less than 100, so there is enough money. The branch is not taken.

51
00:07:50,777 --> 00:08:02,831
Instruction 3 subtracts: x 4 becomes 500 minus 100, which is 400. Only the private copy changes. Memory still holds 500.

52
00:08:03,281 --> 00:08:11,793
Instruction 4 stores x 4. The value 400 travels into memory, so the shared balance is now 400.

53
00:08:12,243 --> 00:08:22,653
Then thread 1 runs the same code. It computes the same address, and its load reads 400, because thread 0 already stored 400.

54
00:08:23,103 --> 00:08:32,575
400 is not less than 100. Thread 1 subtracts: 400 minus 100 is 300, in its own x 4.

55
00:08:33,025 --> 00:08:35,713
Thread 1 stores 300 into memory.

56
00:08:36,163 --> 00:08:46,830
The result is 300: two withdrawals of 100 from 500. It is correct, because thread 1 loaded the balance after thread 0 stored it.

57
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:57,840
This question type gives an order of instructions from two threads and asks for the final value in memory. The problem on screen is from page 14.

58
00:08:58,290 --> 00:09:06,290
The method card has five steps. Step 1: write the instructions in time order, one row for each instruction.

59
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:12,607
Step 2: a load copies the memory value into the register of the thread that runs it.

60
00:09:13,057 --> 00:09:18,945
Step 3: arithmetic changes only that thread's register. Memory does not change.

61
00:09:19,395 --> 00:09:25,453
Step 4: a store copies the register into memory. The old value in memory is lost.

62
00:09:25,903 --> 00:09:31,941
Step 5: compare the final value with the result of running the threads one after the other.

63
00:09:32,391 --> 00:09:35,804
Pause the video and try it: find the final balance.

64
00:09:36,254 --> 00:09:43,059
Here is the solution. Step 1: write the instructions in time order. Thread 0 runs first.

65
00:09:43,509 --> 00:09:47,307
Thread 0 computes the address of the balance in x 3.

66
00:09:47,757 --> 00:09:53,175
Step 2: the load copies 500 from memory into x 4 of thread 0.

67
00:09:53,625 --> 00:09:58,233
500 is not less than 100, so thread 0 goes on.

68
00:09:58,683 --> 00:10:08,518
Step 3: thread 0 subtracts in its own register. 500 minus 100 is 400. Memory still holds 500.

69
00:10:08,968 --> 00:10:16,733
Now an interrupt stops thread 0 before its store. The value 400 waits in x 4 of thread 0.

70
00:10:17,183 --> 00:10:22,346
Thread 1 runs. It computes the same address, so it uses the same balance.

71
00:10:22,796 --> 00:10:31,201
Step 2 again: thread 1 loads the balance. Memory still holds 500, so thread 1 also gets 500.

72
00:10:31,651 --> 00:10:43,086
500 is not less than 100, so thread 1 goes on. Step 3: it subtracts in its own x 4: 500 minus 100 is 400.

73
00:10:43,536 --> 00:10:49,424
Step 4: thread 1 stores 400. The balance in memory becomes 400.

74
00:10:49,874 --> 00:10:58,813
Thread 0 continues with instruction 4. It stores its old copy, 400, and overwrites memory with 400.

75
00:10:59,263 --> 00:11:08,692
Step 5: compare. One after the other, the result is 300. Here it is 400, so one withdrawal of 100 is lost.

76
00:11:09,142 --> 00:11:16,950
The cause: both threads loaded 500 before either stored. The fix is to synchronize access to the balance.

77
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:27,469
The fix is synchronization. It regulates access to shared data, so that only one thread at a time uses it. This is mutual exclusion.

78
00:11:27,919 --> 00:11:37,263
The low-level tool is a lock. Higher-level tools are called semaphores or mutexes. The code adds a shared variable named lock.

79
00:11:37,713 --> 00:11:42,001
A lock has two operations: acquire lock, and release lock.

80
00:11:42,451 --> 00:11:50,003
The code between acquire and release is the critical section. Here it holds the test and the debit of the balance.

81
00:11:50,453 --> 00:11:58,880
Acquire and release must interleave. Thread 0 acquires the lock, runs its critical section, and releases the lock.

82
00:11:59,330 --> 00:12:07,949
If thread 1 calls acquire while thread 0 holds the lock, acquire blocks. Thread 1 enters only after the release.

83
00:12:08,399 --> 00:12:12,793
Rule: only one thread at a time can be between acquire and release.

84
00:12:13,243 --> 00:12:24,934
Problem from page 16: the same order of instructions, but now each thread calls acquire before its critical section and release after it. Pause the video and try it.

85
00:12:25,384 --> 00:12:35,816
Solution, step 1: write the rows in time order. Thread 0 calls acquire. The lock is 0, so thread 0 gets it and lock becomes 1.

86
00:12:36,266 --> 00:12:42,666
Thread 0 computes the address and loads the balance: 500 travels into its x 4.

87
00:12:43,116 --> 00:12:51,457
500 is not less than 100, so thread 0 subtracts in its own register: x 4 becomes 400.

88
00:12:51,907 --> 00:12:58,542
An interrupt stops thread 0 inside its critical section. Thread 0 still holds the lock.

89
00:12:58,992 --> 00:13:06,843
Thread 1 calls acquire. The lock is 1, so thread 1 spins. It waits in acquire and does not touch the balance.

90
00:13:07,293 --> 00:13:10,258
Another interrupt switches back to thread 0.

91
00:13:10,708 --> 00:13:15,956
Thread 0 stores 400. The balance in memory becomes 400.

92
00:13:16,406 --> 00:13:20,054
Thread 0 calls release, so lock becomes 0.

93
00:13:20,504 --> 00:13:28,163
Thread 1 is still in acquire. Now it sees lock equal to 0 and gets the lock, so lock becomes 1 again.

94
00:13:28,613 --> 00:13:35,759
Thread 1 loads the balance. Memory holds 400, so 400 travels into its x 4.

95
00:13:36,209 --> 00:13:44,145
400 is not less than 100, so thread 1 subtracts: 400 minus 100 is 300.

96
00:13:44,595 --> 00:13:47,283
Thread 1 stores 300 into memory.

97
00:13:47,733 --> 00:13:51,040
Thread 1 calls release, and lock becomes 0.

98
00:13:51,490 --> 00:14:00,535
Step 5: compare. The result is 300, the same as one after the other. The lock made the critical sections take turns.

99
00:14:00,985 --> 00:14:11,375
Now build acquire and release. The first idea is a spin lock in software. Acquire waits while lock is not 0, then sets lock to 1.

100
00:14:11,825 --> 00:14:17,649
A0 loads lock into x 6. Lock is 0 here, so x 6 gets 0.

101
00:14:18,099 --> 00:14:28,189
A1 branches back to A0 while x 6 is not 0. So the thread spins while lock is 1. Here x 6 is 0, so it goes on.

102
00:14:28,639 --> 00:14:33,205
A2 adds 1: x 6 becomes 0 plus 1, which is 1.

103
00:14:33,655 --> 00:14:39,585
A3 stores x 6 into lock. Lock is now 1, so this thread holds the lock.

104
00:14:40,035 --> 00:14:46,009
Release stores x 0, which always holds 0, into lock. The lock is free again.

105
00:14:46,459 --> 00:14:56,016
Next, check if this acquire works. The test in A0 and A1 and the set in A3 are separate instructions, so atomicity matters.

106
00:14:56,466 --> 00:15:05,405
This question type gives the code of a lock and asks if it works. The problem on screen uses the spin lock of page 17.

107
00:15:05,855 --> 00:15:11,849
Step 1: find the test, which is the load of lock, and the set, which is the store to lock.

108
00:15:12,299 --> 00:15:18,678
Step 2: run thread 0 through its test. It sees lock equal to 0, so it will enter.

109
00:15:19,128 --> 00:15:26,232
Step 3: switch to thread 1 before thread 0 runs its set. Thread 1's test also sees 0.

110
00:15:26,682 --> 00:15:32,997
Step 4: both threads set lock to 1, and both enter the critical section. The lock fails.

111
00:15:33,447 --> 00:15:40,956
Step 5: if the test and the set are one atomic instruction, no switch fits between them, and the lock works.

112
00:15:41,406 --> 00:15:45,310
Pause the video and try it: find the order of instructions.

113
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:55,488
Solution. Step 1: the test is A0 and A1, the load and the branch. The set is A3, the store. Lock starts at 0.

114
00:15:55,938 --> 00:16:03,554
Step 2: run thread 0 through its test. A0 loads lock, so x 6 of thread 0 gets 0.

115
00:16:04,004 --> 00:16:09,103
A1: x 6 is 0, so thread 0 does not loop. It will enter.

116
00:16:09,553 --> 00:16:17,851
Step 3: switch to thread 1 before thread 0's set. A0 of thread 1 loads lock, and lock is still 0.

117
00:16:18,301 --> 00:16:23,016
A1 of thread 1 also sees 0, so thread 1 will also enter.

118
00:16:23,466 --> 00:16:28,095
Step 4: thread 0 runs A2, so its x 6 becomes 1.

119
00:16:28,545 --> 00:16:34,007
Thread 0 runs A3: lock becomes 1. Thread 0 holds the lock.

120
00:16:34,457 --> 00:16:40,601
Thread 1 runs A2. Its x 6 becomes 1, because its copy of lock was 0.

121
00:16:41,051 --> 00:16:45,957
Thread 1 runs A3. It stores 1 into lock, which is already 1.

122
00:16:46,407 --> 00:16:49,202
Thread 0 enters the critical section.

123
00:16:49,652 --> 00:16:56,052
Thread 1 also enters the critical section. Both threads are inside, so the lock failed.

124
00:16:56,502 --> 00:17:06,721
The cause: the loads and stores of two acquire sequences can interleave. The acquire is not atomic, so we have the same problem as before.

125
00:17:07,171 --> 00:17:18,755
A correct implementation puts acquire in a system call, an OS call. Only the kernel can control the interleaving, because only the kernel can disable interrupts.

126
00:17:19,205 --> 00:17:29,018
A1 disables interrupts. No interrupt can switch threads now, so A2 to A5, the test and the set, run as one atomic block.

127
00:17:29,468 --> 00:17:40,476
A6 enables interrupts again before the return. If the lock was busy, A3 jumps to A0, which enables interrupts, so other threads can run.

128
00:17:40,926 --> 00:17:49,971
If an interrupt arrives inside the block, it is taken only after A6. So no switch can fall between the test and the set.

129
00:17:50,421 --> 00:17:56,693
It works, but it is inefficient: every acquire pays the large overhead of a system call.

130
00:17:57,143 --> 00:18:05,805
A better spin lock uses an atomic instruction of the ISA. An example is atomic swap, written A M O swap.

131
00:18:06,255 --> 00:18:17,519
A M O swap x 1, lock, does three things as one: copy x 1 into a temporary x 2, load lock into x 1, and store x 2 into lock.

132
00:18:17,969 --> 00:18:24,817
The new acquire puts 1 into x 1 first. Case 1: the lock is free, so lock holds 0.

133
00:18:25,267 --> 00:18:30,003
First, the old x 1, which is 1, goes into the temporary x 2.

134
00:18:30,453 --> 00:18:33,674
Then lock, which is 0, goes into x 1.

135
00:18:34,124 --> 00:18:38,199
Then the temporary 1 goes into lock. The lock is now 1.

136
00:18:38,649 --> 00:18:46,350
A1 tests x 1. It is 0, so the lock was free. This thread now holds the lock and leaves the loop.

137
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:56,229
Case 2: the lock is busy, so lock holds 1. The swap puts 1 into x 1 and 1 into lock, so the lock does not change.

138
00:18:56,679 --> 00:19:08,199
A1 sees x 1 equal to 1, so the thread keeps looping. Rule: the swap reads and sets the lock in one instruction, so at most one thread holds the lock.

139
00:19:08,649 --> 00:19:23,497
Problem from page 23: both threads run the new acquire. The question is whether the lock works. Step 1: the test and the set are both inside A0, the A M O swap. Pause the video and try it.

140
00:19:23,947 --> 00:19:31,307
Step 2: thread 0 runs A0. The swap loads lock, which is 0, into x 1 of thread 0.

141
00:19:31,757 --> 00:19:37,837
In the same instruction, the old x 1, which is 1, goes into lock. Lock is now 1.

142
00:19:38,287 --> 00:19:44,218
A1: x 1 of thread 0 is 0, so thread 0 leaves the loop with the lock.

143
00:19:44,668 --> 00:19:50,961
Step 3: switch to thread 1. Its swap loads lock, which is already 1, into its x 1.

144
00:19:51,411 --> 00:19:57,961
In the same instruction, thread 1's old x 1, which is 1, goes into lock. Lock stays 1.

145
00:19:58,411 --> 00:20:01,184
Thread 0 runs its critical section.

146
00:20:01,634 --> 00:20:06,775
A1 of thread 1: x 1 is 1, so thread 1 loops back to A0.

147
00:20:07,225 --> 00:20:11,492
Thread 1 swaps again. It gets 1 again, and lock stays 1.

148
00:20:11,942 --> 00:20:16,251
Thread 1 keeps spinning until thread 0 releases the lock.

149
00:20:16,701 --> 00:20:27,133
Step 5: no switch can fall between the test and the set, because they are one instruction. The lock works. It is often called a test and set lock.

150
00:20:27,583 --> 00:20:38,591
Building an atomic swap in hardware is not easy. No other memory operation may come between the load and the store, so other threads must be blocked for a moment.

151
00:20:39,041 --> 00:20:49,217
Also, a load and a store in one instruction is not very RISC-like. One approach is an instruction pair: load reserved and store conditional.

152
00:20:49,667 --> 00:20:57,454
Load reserved, L R, loads lock into x 1. It also remembers the address of lock as reserved.

153
00:20:57,904 --> 00:21:05,221
In this example, thread 1 also runs L R. It also loads 0, and it also reserves lock.

154
00:21:05,671 --> 00:21:19,645
Store conditional, S C, checks for a change at the reserved address. Lock has not changed since thread 1's L R, so the store happens: 1 goes into lock, and S C returns 0 for success.

155
00:21:20,095 --> 00:21:24,105
Lock changed, so the reservation of thread 0 is lost.

156
00:21:24,555 --> 00:21:35,414
Thread 0's S C sees the change. It does not store, and it returns 1 for failure. So thread 0 must try again. Only one thread got the lock.

157
00:21:35,864 --> 00:21:45,123
The test and set lock works, but it performs poorly. Take 3 processors. Processor 2 holds the lock and runs its critical section.

158
00:21:45,573 --> 00:21:55,599
Processors 0 and 1 wait in acquire. Each loop runs an A M O swap, and every A M O swap writes lock: it includes a store.

159
00:21:56,049 --> 00:22:02,407
So every loop puts a store on the interconnect. Here P 0 swaps, then P 1 swaps.

160
00:22:02,857 --> 00:22:10,473
They keep spinning. With 3 loops each, that is 6 stores while P 2 holds the lock, and all of them are useless.

161
00:22:10,923 --> 00:22:17,771
Rule: repeated stores by several processors are costly. They make useless interconnect traffic.

162
00:22:18,221 --> 00:22:24,941
The fix is the test and test and set lock. It is not a new instruction, only different software.

163
00:22:25,391 --> 00:22:30,809
A0 and A1 spin with a simple load. The thread loops while lock is 1.

164
00:22:31,259 --> 00:22:41,990
Only when the load sees 0, A2 sets x 1 to 1, and A3 does the A M O swap. A4 loops back if the swap found the lock busy.

165
00:22:42,440 --> 00:22:56,563
A busy lock stays in each processor's own cache, so the spinning loads hit locally. Only the first load of P 0 and of P 1 uses the interconnect: 2 transfers instead of 6 stores.

166
00:22:57,013 --> 00:23:06,591
When the lock changes, the cache block is invalidated, so the waiting processors load the new value. Video 22b shows how.

167
00:23:07,041 --> 00:23:13,505
Rule: spin with a load in your own cache, and do the A M O swap only when the lock looks free.

168
00:23:13,955 --> 00:23:21,742
Back to the simplest multiprocessor: both pipelines share the level 1 caches. This design has two problems.

169
00:23:22,192 --> 00:23:31,259
Every access of every core goes to the same cache. This is a bandwidth and latency problem, and the cores can also thrash the cache.

170
00:23:31,709 --> 00:23:44,061
The solution: each processor gets its own private cache. A cache coherence protocol coordinates the private caches. The rest of this video shows why the protocol is needed.

171
00:23:44,511 --> 00:23:54,580
The programmer's conceptual model is simple: processors P 0 to P 3 share one memory. Life would be easy if systems looked like this.

172
00:23:55,030 --> 00:24:04,374
Real systems look more like this: processors have caches, memory may be physically distributed, and an interconnect links everything.

173
00:24:04,824 --> 00:24:15,661
Back to the bank example. Each withdrawal runs on its own processor, and a lock protects the critical section. First assume the processors have no caches.

174
00:24:16,111 --> 00:24:21,999
Processor 0 loads 500 from memory and stores 400 to memory.

175
00:24:22,449 --> 00:24:34,289
Processor 1 then loads 400 from memory and stores 300. Each load reads memory, so it sees the last store: no problem. Next, add caches.

176
00:24:34,739 --> 00:24:47,838
Scenario 2: one on-chip cache, shared by all processors. P 0, P 1 and P 2 reach it through the interconnect. Memory holds A, the balance, equal to 500.

177
00:24:48,288 --> 00:24:56,928
P 1 loads A. The request goes over the interconnect to the shared cache. The shared cache does not have A, so it is a miss.

178
00:24:57,378 --> 00:25:00,002
So the shared cache asks memory for A.

179
00:25:00,452 --> 00:25:06,596
Memory sends 500. The shared cache keeps a copy: tag A, data 500.

180
00:25:07,046 --> 00:25:12,529
The shared cache sends 500 to P 1, so the load returns 500.

181
00:25:12,979 --> 00:25:19,208
Now P 1 stores 400 to A. The store goes over the interconnect to the shared cache.

182
00:25:19,658 --> 00:25:24,309
The store writes into the shared cache, so its data becomes 400.

183
00:25:24,759 --> 00:25:31,159
The shared cache marks the block dirty. Memory is not updated: it still holds 500.

184
00:25:31,609 --> 00:25:42,681
There is only one copy in the caches, so every core sees 400. But the shared cache is a bottleneck, because every access of every core goes to it.

185
00:25:43,131 --> 00:25:50,619
Now add a private cache to each core. These are write-back caches. They reduce latency and increase throughput.

186
00:25:51,069 --> 00:25:58,173
P 1 loads A. It misses in its own cache and in the shared cache, so the request goes on to memory.

187
00:25:58,623 --> 00:26:06,793
Memory sends 500. The shared cache keeps A equal to 500, marked clean, because it matches memory.

188
00:26:07,243 --> 00:26:13,323
The shared cache sends 500 up to P 1's cache, and the load returns 500.

189
00:26:13,773 --> 00:26:22,179
P 1 stores 400. A write-back cache writes only its own copy, so P 1's cache now holds 400.

190
00:26:22,629 --> 00:26:28,239
P 1 marks its block dirty. The shared cache and memory still hold 500.

191
00:26:28,689 --> 00:26:35,068
Now P 0 loads A. It misses in its own cache, so the request goes to the shared cache.

192
00:26:35,518 --> 00:26:41,555
The shared cache has A equal to 500, so it sends 500 to P 0's cache.

193
00:26:42,005 --> 00:26:50,539
P 0 gets 500, but the last store wrote 400 into P 1's cache. P 0 got the wrong balance.

194
00:26:50,989 --> 00:26:58,135
Rule: with private write-back caches, a load can return a stale value. The caches are incoherent.

195
00:26:58,585 --> 00:27:10,724
This question type gives the caches and a list of loads and stores, and asks which value a load returns. The problem uses private write-through caches, from page 48.

196
00:27:11,174 --> 00:27:15,718
Step 1: look in the core's own cache. A hit returns that copy.

197
00:27:16,168 --> 00:27:20,840
Step 2: on a miss, ask the shared cache. On a miss there, ask memory.

198
00:27:21,290 --> 00:27:27,626
Step 3: a write-back store changes only the core's own cache, and marks the block dirty.

199
00:27:28,076 --> 00:27:35,223
Step 4: a write-through store also writes the shared cache and memory. It never writes another private cache.

200
00:27:35,673 --> 00:27:41,305
Step 5: the value is stale if a newer store is not in the place where the load reads.

201
00:27:41,755 --> 00:27:46,405
Pause the video and try it: what does the last load of P 1 return?

202
00:27:46,855 --> 00:27:55,197
Here is the solution. The caches are write-through now. All caches start empty, and memory holds A equal to 500.

203
00:27:55,647 --> 00:28:03,540
Steps 1 and 2: P 1 loads A. It misses in its own cache and in the shared cache, so the request goes to memory.

204
00:28:03,990 --> 00:28:13,761
Memory sends 500. It travels up to P 1's cache, and the shared cache also keeps a copy. The load returns 500.

205
00:28:14,211 --> 00:28:20,867
P 1 stores 400. Step 4: a write-through store first writes P 1's own cache.

206
00:28:21,317 --> 00:28:28,314
Then the store goes through: 400 travels down to memory, and the shared cache also gets 400.

207
00:28:28,764 --> 00:28:35,676
P 0 loads A. Step 1: it misses in its own cache, so the request goes to the shared cache.

208
00:28:36,126 --> 00:28:42,846
The shared cache holds 400, so P 0 gets 400. This time the value is right.

209
00:28:43,296 --> 00:28:47,584
P 0 stores 300. It writes its own cache first.

210
00:28:48,034 --> 00:28:52,962
Then the store goes through: memory and the shared cache now hold 300.

211
00:28:53,412 --> 00:28:59,364
P 1 loads A again. Step 1: P 1's own cache still has A, so it is a hit.

212
00:28:59,814 --> 00:29:07,323
Step 5: the hit returns 400, but the last store wrote 300. P 1 read a stale value.

213
00:29:07,773 --> 00:29:16,883
Rule: write-through updates memory, but never the other private caches. So a hit in a private cache can still return a stale value.

214
00:29:17,333 --> 00:29:25,951
There are four options. Option 1: no caches. Then every access goes to memory: correct, but slow and unacceptable.

215
00:29:26,401 --> 00:29:35,681
Option 2: do not cache shared data. Private data stays fast, but it is still too slow, because the whole accts database is shared.

216
00:29:36,131 --> 00:29:43,449
Option 3: flush all other caches on every write to shared data. Then we may as well not have caches.

217
00:29:43,899 --> 00:29:59,237
The solution is hardware cache coherence. The rough goal: all caches have the same data at all times. Minimal flushing and maximum caching give the best performance. Video 22b shows the protocols.

218
00:29:59,687 --> 00:30:10,674
Recap. Threads share memory, and each thread has its own PC, registers and stack. All three hardware ways to run threads give the shared memory model.

219
00:30:11,124 --> 00:30:21,641
A load copies shared data into a private register, and a store overwrites memory. So an interleaving can lose an update. Trace it row by row.

220
00:30:22,091 --> 00:30:33,334
A lock makes the critical section mutually exclusive. Its test and its set must be atomic, with A M O swap or with load reserved and store conditional.

221
00:30:33,784 --> 00:30:41,101
The test and test and set lock spins with loads in its own cache, and swaps only when the lock looks free.

222
00:30:41,551 --> 00:30:54,309
Private caches keep copies. With write-back or write-through caches, a load can return a stale value. So the hardware needs cache coherence, the topic of video 22b.
